#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:116
msgid "Community"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:118
msgid ""
"Community in ROS is the global network of developers, researchers, "
"companies, and contributors who help our library of open-source software "
"grow and improve. People can contribute to plumbing, tools, and "
"capabilities, and thanks to the community, it is easy to share code, "
"exchange ideas, and work together on projects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:121
msgid ""
":doc:`Learn more about community contributions and how you can get involved "
"<The-ROS2-Project/Contributing>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:124
msgid "Integrations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:126
msgid ""
"ROS works with other Open Robotics platforms to make development and "
"deployment easier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:128
msgid ""
"`Gazebo`_: Offers physics-based simulation, so developers can test robots in "
"a virtual environment before using real hardware."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:129
msgid ""
"`Open-RMF`_ (Robotics Middleware Framework): Helps different robots work "
"together and interact with building systems like lifts and doors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:130
msgid "`ros-controls`_: Enables real-time control of robots using ROS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:136
msgid ""
"These integrations make it simpler to design, test, and manage robots in "
"complex environments on any budget and with any team size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:138
msgid ":doc:`Learn more about integrations with ROS<Integrations-and-related>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:141
msgid "ROS distributions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:143
msgid ""
"A ROS distribution is a packaged set of ROS software released on a regular "
"schedule. Each distribution provides a stable version of the core libraries "
"and tools, plus many community packages. This makes it easier for developers "
"to work with a consistent codebase and keep projects compatible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:147
msgid "ROS has two main versions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:149
msgid "`ROS 1`_: The original framework."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:150
msgid ":doc:`ROS 2<Releases>`: The current and actively developed version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:154
msgid ""
"ROS 1 has reached the end of development, with no new releases planned, "
"while ROS 2 continues to be developed and releases new distributions every "
"year. These distributions include updates and improvements, so developers "
"can choose between stability and the latest features."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:157
msgid ":doc:`Learn more about ROS 2 releases<Releases>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:160
msgid "Supported systems"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:162
msgid ""
"ROS runs on Ubuntu, Windows, and macOS, but we strongly recommend that you "
"use a Tier 1 platform for your ROS distribution. ROS on macOS is supported "
"by the community, and we do not recommended it for new users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:165
msgid ""
"Ubuntu support depends on the ROS distribution, with each distribution "
"requiring a specific Ubuntu LTS (long-term support) release. Other platforms "
"may require building from source or using containers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/About-ROS.rst:168
msgid ""
":ref:`See full details of currently supported platforms and support tiers "
"<CurrentSupportedPlatforms>`"
msgstr ""
